---
import Plate from '../../plate/Plate.astro';
import FilmControls from '../../plate/FilmControls.astro';
import FilmSubtitles from '../../plate/FilmSubtitles.astro';
import FilmTranscript from '../../plate/FilmTranscript.astro';
import '../../plate/film.css';
import { renderFormula } from '../../../lib/formulas/render';
import { TILING_EDGES } from './math';
import { CUES, TIMELINE } from './story';
import './boundary-sphere.css';

const formula = (tex: string) => renderFormula(tex, false);

const labels = [
  { id: 'weight', tex: String.raw`\frac1m`, blue: false },
  { id: 'radius', tex: 'a^{-n}', blue: false },
  { id: 'cost', tex: 'M(n)', blue: true },
];

const tilingPath = TILING_EDGES.map((edge) => edge.points.map((point, index) => `${index === 0 ? 'M' : 'L'}${150 + point.x * 122} ${150 - point.y * 122}`).join('')).join('');

const cells = Array.from({ length: 9 }, (_, index) => ({ x: 35 + index % 3 * 75, y: 45 + Math.floor(index / 3) * 62 }));
---

<Plate
  caption="A group drawn as a network of moves, the sphere at infinity where its paths end, why topology alone is not roundness, and how the approved proof keeps the cost of blocking paths bounded, as on a square grid."
  leftOut="the hyperbolicity constant; visual metrics, quasisymmetry and conformal dimension; the earlier alternative routes; crossing duality, path envelopes and Hölder continuity; the sweep and puncture count; Sullivan–Tukia and the measurable Riemann mapping theorem; the finite kernel and orientation reversal; the fibering corollaries and Lean ledger. The proof drawings are schematic."
>
  <uruk-boundary-sphere class="film-host">
    <div class="bfilm">
      <div class="bfilm-stage" data-film-viewport>
        <canvas class="bfilm-canvas" data-film-canvas role="img" aria-label="A film about groups as networks, their directions to infinity, Cannon's sphere-boundary question, and the approved proof's bound on the cost of blocking paths. A complete text transcript follows."></canvas>
        <div class="bfilm-still" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="bfilm-still-disk">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 300 310" role="presentation">
              <text x="150" y="15" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="var(--font-special-elite)" font-size="10">HYPERBOLIC SPACE · DRAWN IN 2D</text>
              <circle cx="150" cy="150" r="122" fill="none" stroke="var(--muted)" />
              <path d={tilingPath} fill="none" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width=".65" />
              <text x="150" y="291" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--muted)" font-family="var(--font-alegreya)" font-size="12">The edge is infinitely far away.</text>
              <text x="150" y="307" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--muted)" font-family="var(--font-alegreya)" font-size="12">In three dimensions, the boundary is a sphere.</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div class="bfilm-still-grid">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 300 310" role="presentation">
              <text x="150" y="15" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="var(--font-special-elite)" font-size="10">A GRID PATCH · WEIGHT 1/3 PER CELL</text>
              {cells.map((cell) => (
                <>
                  <rect x={cell.x} y={cell.y} width="75" height="62" fill="none" stroke="var(--muted)" stroke-width=".8" />
                  <text x={cell.x + 37.5} y={cell.y + 37} text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="var(--font-alegreya)" font-size="20">⅓</text>
                </>
              ))}
              <line x1="22" y1="154" x2="272" y2="154" stroke="var(--blue)" stroke-width="2" />
              <path d="M265 150L272 154L265 158" fill="none" stroke="var(--blue)" stroke-width="2" />
              <text x="150" y="257" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--blue)" font-family="var(--font-alegreya)" font-size="15">Every crossing collects at least 1.</text>
              <text x="150" y="285" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="var(--font-special-elite)" font-size="11">COST: 9 × (1/3)² = 1</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="bfilm-formulas" aria-hidden="true" data-bleed>
          {labels.map((label) => <span class:list={['bfilm-label', 'bfilm-label--small', { 'bfilm-label--blue': label.blue }]} data-label={label.id}><span set:html={formula(label.tex)} /></span>)}
          <span class="bfilm-label bfilm-energy" data-label="energy"><span>ENERGY → 0</span></span>
          <span class="bfilm-label bfilm-status" data-label="status"><span>APPROVED</span></span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <FilmSubtitles cues={CUES} />
    </div>
    <FilmControls id="bfilm-timeline" timeline={TIMELINE} />
    <FilmTranscript cues={CUES}>
      <p class="film-method">How the film is made. The free-group tree contains 485 vertices through depth five, or 161 through depth four on phones. Its angular sectors leave gaps to show a finite sample of its Cantor boundary. The square-grid triangle has thickness <span set:html={formula('n')} /> in the graph metric. The pentagon tiling is generated by reflecting a right-angled pentagon across its geodesic sides. The base patch has 166 tiles. An inverse-translated copy supplies additional tiles while the final move carries the patch across the disk. The final translation by twice one side length is a symmetry of the infinite tiling.</p>
      <p class="film-method">The disk illustrates hyperbolic geometry in two dimensions. This tiled plane and its group have a circle boundary, not the sphere boundary required by Cannon's theorem. The line globe stands for that sphere. The boundary magnification uses an exact hyperbolic translation; its use as a particular group element is schematic. Intermediate positions of the final symmetry need not themselves be elements of the tiling group.</p>
      <p class="film-method">On an <span set:html={formula(String.raw`m\times m`)} /> grid, assigning weight <span set:html={formula(String.raw`\frac1m`)} /> to every cell gives every crossing weight at least one and squared cost exactly one. This is an admissible bound, so the minimum cost cannot be larger. The crumpled sphere, coverings, irregular patch, function, copied ranges and value tree are schematic. The last graph uses illustrative constants in <span set:html={formula('2^{n/2}')} /> and <span set:html={formula('50(5+4n)^2')} />, whose first integer crossing is at 41. The film illustrates the proof without checking it. Its approval label follows the article's account of Bonk's qualified assessment.</p>
    </FilmTranscript>
  </uruk-boundary-sphere>
</Plate>

<script>
  import './element';
</script>
